{
  "origin": "A `failed` executor used to be offered a way out of the task: the gate carried skip and stop beside the retry, and the executor's own ISSUES were echoed under a header as the whole of what the user got to judge it on. During the build there is no way out — a task is finished or being fixed — so the gate is the retry and Comment alone, and what stands above it is a composed failure block: what failed, what was tried, why, and what the next attempt should do differently. This walks that: the executor cannot make the capture consumer's idempotency test pass, the session reads the cause against the specification and the code the previous task left, the user retries on that reading, and the **same** executor carries it and finishes. A walk that dispatches a fresh executor has thrown away everything the first one learned, which is the reason the retry is a continuation at all.",
